    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Congressman Joe Barton is the ranking member on the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.",
        "confidence": 0.98
      },
      {
        "text": "There will likely be a debate or discussion in Congress about global warming.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that congressional action on climate issues is possible and meaningful.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes that Congressman Barton has relevant expertise due to his committee position.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views disagreement about climate change as legitimate but significant enough to warrant formal legislative attention.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts that public discourse in Congress reflects a serious consideration of scientific evidence.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 91580,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91580.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Well, first we have to determine what the facts are. Let's get to science and to the extent there is science. You know, I certainly support research. I certainly accept that the temperature since 1850 is warmed generically, but I do not accept that it's a given that the temperature is in an escalating climate temperature increase because of human-caused emissions from electricity generation and tailpipe emissions and things like that. The science does not support that. In fact, there's quite a bit of science that says the Earth goes through naturally reoccurring warming and cooling periods. And the amount of greenhouse gas is, as a percent of the total part of the atmosphere, so insignificant, like about one-tenth of one-thousandth percent, that it's just way too early to say that that A, we're on an automatically increasing climate temperature increase, no matter what, and B, that whatever the temperature is going up or down, that it's caused by mankind. So I think we ought to continue to do the research, continue to do the science, continue to do things that make sense economically, but not devastate our economy by putting some mandatory carbon capture and control system that just sends jobs overseas to China and India.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The temperature has increased since 1850.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Continued research into climate change is valuable.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Human activities such as electricity generation and tailpipe emissions contribute to greenhouse gas emissions.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Greenhouse gases constitute approximately 0.0001% of the atmosphere.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "It is premature to conclude that global temperature increases are due to human-caused emissions.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Mandatory carbon capture and control systems would harm the economy.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "There is scientific evidence supporting natural reoccurring warming and cooling cycles.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Such systems could lead to job losses and offshoring to countries like China and India.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Economic stability takes precedence over immediate environmental regulation.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Climate science is still uncertain enough to warrant caution before implementing large-scale policy changes.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
