  {
    "episode_id": 91580,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91580.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 51,
    "turn_text": "I might mention that 80 to 90 percent of the violence is in and around Baghdad, and so these troops leaving southern Iraq, the hope is, the thought is, that they are not going to affect the violent situation. We don't have Sunnis fighting. She is there. There may be some increase in the Shia on Shia fighting, and that will have to work itself out in the Iraqis. We'll have to get control of that situation. But the British troops are going to stay until at least 2008 says Tony Blair. He will leave troops there. And again, as I said, most non-U.S. troops are concentrated in southern Iraq. And our troop surge is in Baghdad and the Anbar provinces. If you have any comments on this, do you think this is a good idea? Or do you think this is an ominous announcement today by Tony Blair. Give us a call at 888-192-70. I think that the effect is really political here, not strategic. I think this could hurt the Bush administration because of those who are saying that this is a way that the international community is isolating our efforts there and that they are not with us any longer. Do you agree with that? Do you think that is the case? Of course, kind of Lisa Rice saying that this is consistent with the plan to turn over more control to the Iraqi forces and that this is the eventual plan for the country as a whole, Dick Cheney saying the same thing, Bush saying this is a sign of success in Iraq. We'd love to hear from you on this issue. Again, give us a call, 800-881-9270. But I want to transition to another issue, something that's been in the news this week and we haven't gotten to cover yet. And by the way, my thanks to Kirby Anderson for sitting in yesterday and doing such a great job as he always does. and to Andrew Abert on Monday and to Denny Burke on Friday while Dr. Johnson and I were both at the National Religious Broadcasters Convention. I saw him a couple of times there, but it's such a big convention conference. There are thousands and thousands of broadcasters there, so I didn't see much of him. But it was a great, great time and we'll talk a little bit more about that later. But I want to mention that in the state of New Jersey, This week, Civil Unions began, and Civil Unions are, of course, marriage without the name. They're the benefits of marriage for homosexual couples without calling it marriage. This began in the state of New Jersey because of a court decision, and it's really, they're now legal, and I want to get a report now from AP Correspondent Jeff Mulville.",
