  {
    "episode_id": 91580,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91580.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 82,
    "turn_text": "Well, it certainly is impending on my right to take to provide health care for my daughter. Now, certainly I can get a waiver to not have to have my child vaccinated. I don't even know totally what that process is. I've been told that it's a little bit difficult and that it has to be notarized. So that makes it a little bit harder. So unless somebody is, you know, really understands that there is a way to get out of taking the vaccine, then they're going to be, you know, the school is going to hold that over their head. If you don't get this vaccine, then you can't come to school. And it's just really a difficult situation to help parents understand that maybe they could, you know, bypass that situation. But my understanding is even some schools in some areas won't really allow you to opt out. So, but the main reason that we're upset about this, at least I am, is just that there's a very little experience with this vaccine. It's only been out about eight months. We've never mandated a vaccine that early. The earliest that I know is the chickenpox vaccine, which is about five years. And that was, you know, after extensive experience with the vaccine, And that's a disease that you can get by, you know, just",
